  <h2>What is Magisk?</h2>
  <p>
    Magisk is an open-source rooting framework created by topjohnwu. It is called
    "systemless" because it patches the device's boot image instead of modifying
    the system partition: the operating-system files stay untouched, while root
    access becomes available once the patched image boots.
  </p>

  <h2>What it gives you</h2>
  <p>
    Superuser (root) access with per-app prompts and logging; installable modules
    that change system behavior without editing <code>/system</code>; Zygisk, which
    runs module code inside the Android runtime; and the DenyList, which keeps
    selected apps away from Magisk's modifications.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you root</h2>
  <p>
    Magisk requires an unlocked bootloader on virtually all devices, and rooting
    trips integrity checks that some apps — banking and payment apps above all —
    actively look for. The Magisk app itself warns: download Magisk
    <em>only</em> from the official GitHub page; files from unknown sources can be
    malicious. Knowing your exact device and bootloader state is step one — our
    free <a href="/scan">browser-based scan</a> reports both over USB.
  </p>
